i'm scarlet foo this is bloomberg quick  take  a powerful storm that ripped across the  midwest and great plains with hurricane  force winds killed at least five people  the powerful system spawned tornadoes  toppling trees and leaving nearly 500  000 homes and businesses without power  winds gusted to 90 miles per hour with  temperatures unseasonably warm the  national weather service says 21  tornadoes were reported with at least 19  outbreaks of hail some of which reached  the size of a softball and the system  came on the heels of devastating  tornadoes last weekend that cut a path  through kentucky and of course  neighboring states as well this killed  more than 85 people american red cross  spokesperson kelly edwards joins us  joining us now from kentucky for an  update callie thank you for being here  and glad to see that you're okay i know  you've been on the ground give us a  sense of the situation what are you  seeing and how bad is it  so for the past couple of days i've been  in the city of mayfield kentucky  surveying the damage and talking with um  residents and it is it is something that  i have never seen before  in 10 years of serving with the american  red cross the devastation it just  looks  so immense compared to some of the other  tornado responses i have served on  but what is clear is that the residents  and the community are coming together to  find ways to help this these communities  recover  that is certainly good to hear um  despite all the damage one thing that  strikes me kelly is that we're getting  better at predicting when and where  tornadoes specifically do strike yet the  damage they incur when they strike is  pretty crippling why is that why haven't  we gotten better at that part of it  well i'm not a client a meteorologist so  i can't speak to weather  specifically like that but one of the  things that we do know with the red  cross is that these types of disasters  continue to happen they happen more  often and one of the things that the red  cross as we try to be prepared and try  to prepare communities we encourage  individuals to also be prepared to know  the signs of danger and heed those  warnings  with tornadoes you often only get a  couple of seconds or minutes to respond  so being able to have those emergency  alerts in your community or from your  red cross emergency app can help you  take cover  i spoke with one resident that um had  heard the  the tornado alert and she immediately  ran into her bathroom and took shelter  in her bathtub and when she recovered  when her neighbors came to check on her  she found that her home was in pieces  and so  the time that people take to take those  steps to take cover can make a  difference and save lives and i know  that you're in kentucky where 110 people  were working in a candle factory when a  tornado came through and of course 90  were found in the rubble of that factory  after the fact  that example just makes me wonder to  what extent the victims that you've  heard of tend to be coming from  individuals like the one you mentioned  who are in their houses at the time or  is a lot of it people who were actually  at work and their employers kept them  there  so i can't speak to companies and the  employee situations like that  what i can speak to is that i've spoken  with residents and business community  members that had businesses in downtown  mayfield and they saw that destruction  and they saw their businesses basically  be imploded by this disaster  and so it's been very devastating for  them but they also  are very thankful to be alive they're  thankful for the partners and community  members coming together to help them  start taking those steps towards  recovery  so americans seeing these pictures are  obviously devastated by by what what the  damage that has been done to to all  these people who were in the wrong place  at the wrong time really  can you give us a sense of the donations  that have come in uh to help people in  the region  all right what does the trajectory of  that look like and is it earmarked for  this area this region these storms  specifically  thank you for asking that because i know  people are looking at these images and  wondering how they can help and people  are stepping up and helping to make  financial donations to the red cross and  other partners that are responding in  these disasters financial funds help us  get resources to people and the who need  it and who need it in place and where  it's needed most the most quickly  also people can also make blood  donations in their community to help us  we're within we're in a national blood  shortage at this time we've had the  lowest blood supply in in a decade and  so donating blood is a great way in a  free way that individuals can commute  contribute and help i wonder how kovid  comes into play with this is that one  element that's made it difficult to get  the blood donations needed to help the  survivors in the area  absolutely now one of the things i will  say the red cross  and as far as related to the tornadoes  the red cross provided over 200 units of  blood  for  hospitals in this area to help with the  disaster response  red cross as a national blood bank we're  able to get blood products where they're  needed most  one of the keys is that  people that donate today and tomorrow  help make sure blood is on the shelves  when these emergencies happen and  hospital patients need it when they get  it when they need it most  can you kelly give us a sense of where  we are in the disaster relief effort  because i know there tends to be stages  in in how this rolls out where are we in  this are we early on uh in the early  innings middle innings  we are we are still early on in this  response i can tell you on wednesday  night hundreds of people were still  spending the night in 10 um cross and  community partner shelters across the  states  the red cross we we have provided over  seven or over 7 000 meals and snacks at  this point  even like a day like today where we're  experiencing some severe weather in  kentucky our volunteers and our partners  are still sheltering feeding helping to  provide that  those resources and comfort  i will also say that our team our  volunteers are also reaching out to the  people that have been affected by these  by these storms and that have lost loved  ones the red cross has disaster  health services mental health and  spiritual care volunteers that are  helping to make touch with these  families and help provide  to their emotional needs after going  through such a devastating event  kelly final question for you when you  look ahead and kind of take a step back  here what are you most concerned about  moving forward is it the kind of  diminishing effect of relief a few  months out from events like this is it  the lack of blood donations that we're  seeing what are you most worried about  so what i would say when these disasters  happen i'm from north carolina i've seen  many tornadoes and many hurricane  disasters in my 10 years in the red  cross  these disasters take time and long after  the  cameras turn away from the disaster  there is still relief efforts that need  are happening in that area this is going  to be a long recovery there's going to  be many partners many people involved in  the recovery of for these communities  and so  it's a busy time everyone is also  focused on their holiday plans and  season but think about these families  and how you can possibly help through a  financial donation or blood donation and  if you're not able to do those  encourage your concur be encouraged to  join us as a volunteer to help in your  community as well  really really helpful thank you callie  edwards spokesperson for the american  red cross joining us from kentucky  where of course the state has suffered  so many recent natural  disasters  cali thank you so much
 

